de we ran into this  ideological wall ironically cap and  trade came out of the American  Enterprise Institute it was a  conservative idea originally because it  was market-based but then the ideologues  grabbed onto it and transformed it into  a better R that is unacceptable because  it's quote command and control  interference of government in the  marketplace now complete flip to talk  about the sophistry of our recent  politics so how did you in California I  know you have a legislature that's  that's of the same party so you've got  uniform ability to move forward but you  obviously ran into these arguments and I  wonder if you'd share with people how  you knock those down or overcame them in  order to institute a cap-and-trade  system in California itself well to  institute it actually that was done by  my predecessor Arnold but Schwarzenegger  had some big ideas high-speed rail  nonpartisan primaries so one of these  was greenhouse gas reduction and he  joined with essentially all the  Democrats I think he got maybe one  Republican vote and he put the first  cap-and-trade program into effect and  then it was to expire in 2020 and the  second round I was able to lead the  effort to get it extended to 2030 and we  did that in part with the help of the  Republican minority leadership who came  in to see me many months ago and said we  want to do something on climb  change also there was a certain amount  of resistance from the Democrats because  there is resistance and they were rather  reluctant so there was another factor  the year before I proposed a 50%  reduction in oil use in California a  very bold move and along with that the  cap on overall greenhouse gases well the  oil companies mobilized to stop the 50%  reduction in oil and they kind of lost  sight of the power of the cap once we  put the cap into place last year then  the companies had to say do we want to  be regulated by bureaucrats in the state  capital or do we want a market-based  system of au