d the Western  dairyman hadn't said we support it they  did say that so you got to get business  that's it and the Democrats and the  academics can only go so far but I think  the point I would make there was a  famous president General Motors named  Charles Wilson Charlie Wilson he said  what's good for General Motors is good  for America now we kind of ridicule that  in the 60s but nevertheless if General  Motors thinks climate action is good for  them then it will be good for America  because America will pick it up because  the the money plays a powerful interest  in our elections billions of dollars I  know I've raised 100 million dollars  myself it's not trivial who those people  are and the Republicans it's also plenty  important so that is kind of  it's pretty simple we know the threat we  know we need business allies we know the  universities and the nonprofit sector  can have a role in influencing all that  and we put it all together you can get  it done it is possible but the the hour  is late and we don't know how far down  the road to an irreversible climate  catastrophe we're headed so well that's  all I'm saying be a little optimistic  but be more activist to get it done and  you can't play a role because you folks  at Yale you all end up on all these big  corporations that's what are you going  whether you know it or not  and you're gonna be in corporate America  and don't forget what you heard today  and please implement it as soon as  possible thank you  J could you speak to the same thing  quickly just sort of share your  challenge now with the you've  promulgated cap-and-trade there's some  people rising in opposition to it  can you give us a sense of that road  ahead and well we have we've had a  different route so I have put it in we  have a cap a limit on the amount of  carbon dioxide that can be emitted in  state of Washington and that was imposed  by my executive order with my stroke of  the pen and pursuant to our state clean  air law so we