nd you have to  believe in it in 1970 when I came back  from Vietnam the first thing I did was  not protest the war the first thing I  did was work for Earth Day 1970 when 20  million Americans came out of their  homes and said we don't want to live  next to toxic waste sites we don't want  to live next to dumps that give you  cancer we do not want to watch rivers  like the Cuyahoga River light on fire we  want to do something about it and guess  what that 20 million people translated  subsequently into political action what  Jay was talking about Jerry so that we  targeted the 12 worst votes in the  United States Congress we labeled them  the Dirty Dozen and in the next election  seven of the twelve members of Congress  lost their seats let me tell you  something there is nothing like a bunch  of your friends  losing their seats to stiffen the spine  of elected officials what happened was  all of a sudden we passed Clean Air Act  Safe Drinking Water Act Marine Mammal  Protection Act Coastal Zone Management  Act we created the EPA and Richard Nixon  that brilliant environmentalists signed  that into law because it was a voting  issue now let me tell you I've had the  privilege of monitoring elections in a  lumber of countries over my career  beginning with the Philippines in 1986  with Cory Aquino and Ferdinand Marcos  and I have watched people in other  countries stand in the hot Sun at risk  sometimes of terrorism go out and vote  in 70 80 90 % numbers we were in the 50s  last year in this election  Jane Donald Trump and Hillary Clinton 50  percent that's just gross  it's unacceptable when I was here at  Yale as an undergraduate we almost had a  nuclear war the Missile Crisis with the  Soviet Union a president United States  was assassinated my sophomore year my  junior year we lined up buses on High  Street and sent people down south at  risk of life to help break the back of  segregation and senior year we  confronted the draft and the issue of  Vietnam and each